course: dictionary
lesson: 217
title: Words from other languages
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Alhamdulillah is not an English word. It is an Arabic phrase that many Muslims say. You can use context clues to find its meaning: «Muslims say «Alhamdulillah», which means «praise be to God», when good things happen.» The sentence tells you the meaning right there, so you do not have to guess.
explain: The phrase is said to thank God. People may say it when they are asked «How are you?», after eating, or when something good happens. It sounds like «al-HAM-doo-li-LAH», and it can also be written al-hamdu lillah.
explain: Other faiths have words of thanks too. The example sentence says Christians may say «Thanks be to God», and Jews say blessings called brachot. Different words can share a similar idea, even when the languages are different.
explain: Respect matters when you learn a word from someone's faith. Say it the way your Muslim friends do, and if you are not sure what a phrase means to them, ask them kindly.
fact: Alhamdulillah is an Arabic phrase, so it is not an English word.
fact: «Alhamdulillah» means «praise be to God» or «thanks be to God».
fact: Muslims say it to thank God for good news and in everyday life.
fact: It is also written al-hamdu lillah, and it sounds like «al-HAM-doo-li-LAH».
fact: It is a key phrase of Islam, and it is also used in religious education lessons.
exercise: What does «Alhamdulillah» mean? | *Praise be to God | Good morning, friend | Well done, you won the game | Goodbye for now
exercise: Which language is alhamdulillah from? | French | Scots | Australian slang | *Arabic
exercise: When might a Muslim say «Alhamdulillah»? | When they are walking through a wet, grey day | *After eating, or when something good happens | When they want to whisper in class | When they lose a game and feel sad
test: Which phrase means the same as «thanks be to God»? | Dinnae | Brachot | Servo | *Alhamdulillah
test: Which of these is an Arabic phrase used by Muslims? | Brachot | *Alhamdulillah | Servo | Dreich
exam: Why is it respectful to ask a friend what a phrase means to them? | Because it is a long word | Because it is a rude word | Because English people never use words from other languages | *Because the phrase is part of their faith and they can explain it
